        {
          "id": 1929631,
          "username": "Muhammad_Saleem_Baba",
          "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/m/97f17d/{size}.png",
          "created_at": "2026-11-02T23:04:25.315Z",
          "cooked": "<p>Do not chase line 1849 in Excel. That number is the line in the file as Shopify parsed it, counting the header row, and it shifts when the file is re-saved. A line break inside a description that is not properly quoted, and trailing blank lines at the end of the file, are the usual reasons the reported numbers run a few lines past the row count you see in a spreadsheet. The last errors, at 1841 to 1849, are simply the last few products in the file. Find them by their Handle and SKU, not by row number.</p>\n<p>The three error types in your report are all variant option problems, and they are fixable in the file itself:</p>\n<p>1. Option is not unique: for one Handle, the same option combination appears more than once. Two rows with the same Handle and the same Option1, Option2 and Option3 values are the same variant to Shopify. It can also happen if an Option Name is repeated, for example Size in both Option1 and Option2. Sort the file by Handle, then by option values, and delete or rename the duplicate combination so every variant under one Handle is unique.</p>\n<p>2. You need to add option values for Size: a row has Option1 Name set to Size but Option1 Value blank. Fill in the size on those rows, or, if the product has no size, remove Size from the Option Name on those rows entirely. A named option with a blank value will always fail.</p>\n<p>3. The variant Default Title already exists: Default Title is only for a product with no real options, and it can appear only once per Handle. If a product has real options such as Size, it must not also have a Default Title row. And if a product is single-variant, its Option1 Name must be Title and its Option1 Value must be Default Title, exactly once. Where you see this error, the Handle already has a Default Title variant, either in the store or earlier in the same file, so the later row is rejected.</p>\n<p>To fix it: open the file in a plain text editor or a spreadsheet, filter to the last products in the file, and check each failing Handle for those three things. Then save the file as UTF-8 CSV, making sure any description that contains a line break or a quote is wrapped in double quotes, with any quote inside it doubled. Then import only the corrected products, with Overwrite products with matching handles checked, and the import email should come back clean for those rows.</p>",
